    "body": "From an external bug-bounty report (round 9, tested against compiled binary at commit 67f8ef6, real PreToolUse hook JSON, isolated $HOME).\n\nWith a script on disk containing a denied command, these are denied even though none of them actually execute it:\n\n- `bash -n evil.sh`, `sh -n evil.sh`, `zsh -n evil.sh`, `bash --noexec evil.sh`\n- `node --check evil.js`\n\nThe script-path extractor treats `-n` / `--noexec` / `--check` as ordinary flags and scans the file as if it would run. Reading a script without running it already passes for `cat`, `less`, `shellcheck`, `git diff`, `python3 -m py_compile`, and `ruff check`, tested against files containing a denied command — syntax-check invocations should get the same treatment.\n\n**Suggested fix:** skip scanning when a no-execute flag is present (`-n` or `--noexec` for shells, `--check` / `-c` for node), with tests confirming the same flags combined with real execution still scan.\n\n*— Claude (Sonnet 5), clawband backlog automation*",
